Mocha (Xiaomi Mi Pad 1, Tegra K1) LineageOS 21 patch set
========================================================

Every change this device tree needs outside of `device/xiaomi/mocha` lives here
as a patch, grouped by the LineageOS source project it belongs to. Nothing in
this directory is built into the image; the scripts apply the series to the
source tree after a `repo sync`.

Layout
------

  patches/<repo path>/NNNN-<slug>.patch

`<repo path>` is the project path inside the Android tree, so
`frameworks/av/*.patch` targets `$ANDROID_TOP/frameworks/av`. `NNNN` is the
apply position inside that project and `-<slug>` repeats the commit subject.
The scripts glob `*.patch`, sort by path and apply in that order, so the numeric
prefix alone decides the order within one project.

Categories
----------

The directory layout is dictated by where a patch applies, so the functional
grouping lives in `patches/categories` instead:

  camera   hardware/interfaces/0001 (camera fences), frameworks/av/0005
           (cameraservice acquire fences - the other half of the same leak)
  media    frameworks/av/0001-0004 (codec2 legacy gralloc, NvMM compat, ACodec
           vendor parameters, OMX YV12 fallback)
  display  frameworks/native/0001 (legacy binder camera interfaces, blackout
           logging, forced GPU composition)
  wifi     hardware/broadcom/wlan, packages/modules/{Wifi,Connectivity}
  system   frameworks/base, lineage-sdk, packages/apps, packages/modules,
           packages/services
  power    bootable/recovery (charger mode display),
           hardware/interfaces/0002 (health HAL 2.1 must live in system)
  build    build/make, system/core, system/sepolicy, prebuilts/gcc,
           vendor/lineage

frameworks/av and frameworks/native used to be personal forks whose
lineage-21.0-mocha branches carried these commits directly. Since 2026-11-28
they are ordinary patches in this series: applying them to the upstream base
fork/lineage-21.0 in a clean worktree reproduces the fork branch tree hash
exactly, so the forks are gone and `.repo/local_manifests` points at the
LineageOSUL projects again. Run install.sh after a repo sync. A full patch path
in `categories` overrides the project prefix for patches that share a project
across categories (used for the two hardware/interfaces patches and for
frameworks/av/0005). List the series with its grouping at any time:

  patches/check.sh --list

Scripts
-------

  patches/check.sh      validate the series without touching any worktree
  patches/install.sh    apply the series (check first, roll back on failure)
  patches/uninstall.sh  reverse only the patches from this series
  patches/lib/patchlib.sh
                        the shared half: locating the Android root, argument
                        parsing, series discovery and category lookup

All three take the same arguments and locate the Android root relative to their
own path, so they can be run from anywhere:

  --only <repo path>   one source project, e.g. --only hardware/interfaces
  --category <name>    one category, e.g. --category camera
  --list               print the series grouped by category and exit

`check.sh` ends with the number of patches in every selected category, and
`install.sh` / `uninstall.sh` print which modules or images each selected
category invalidates, so a run tells you what to rebuild:

  camera   m -j22 camera.tegra
  power    m -j22 charger android.hardware.health@2.1-service
  build    m -j16 otapackage

`check.sh` copies the files the series touches into a scratch tree per project
and applies the patches there with `git apply` (no fuzz), so dependent patches
see the patches before them without changing the source tree or the real index.
Only the touched files are copied, so the whole series checks in about a second
instead of hashing every repository. It prints one of:

  APPLIED   the change is already in the worktree
  READY     applies cleanly, not applied yet
  COVERED   the change is already included by an earlier patch
  CONFLICT  does not apply; the diff is printed

`install.sh` runs `check.sh` with the same filters first and aborts if anything
conflicts. It applies in the same order and, if a patch fails mid-way, reverses
only the patches it applied during that run. `uninstall.sh` reverses in reverse
order and never runs `git checkout` or `git clean`, so local work is preserved.

Source tree dependencies
------------------------

Everything outside `device/xiaomi/mocha` now comes from the LineageOSUL
organisation; the personal forks that used to carry the media/display changes
are gone (see the 2026-11-28 pass below). `.repo/local_manifests/` therefore
only pins `hardware/nvidia/interfaces`.

`build/make` is a dependency that is not a fork: it is a shallow clone in
detached HEAD, so its two patches cannot be committed there. They live here and
are applied to the checkout, which is why `build/make` shows up in this series
even though nothing is pushed anywhere for it.

frameworks/av and frameworks/native used to be personal forks because the
LineageOSUL organisation is read-only for this account
(`permissions.push=false`). Their content is now here:

  frameworks/av      0001-0005   (5 commits, 437 insertions over 14 files)
  frameworks/native  0001        (1 commit, 3 files)

Verified on 2026-11-28: `git worktree add` at the upstream base
`fork/lineage-21.0`, `git apply` the patches, and the resulting tree hash equals
the fork branch's tree hash for both projects - so a repo sync followed by
install.sh produces exactly what the forks used to provide. FMRadio's fork had
no local commits at all and is simply unpinned.

What the patches are for
------------------------

camera (hardware/interfaces, frameworks/av)
  0001  hwc2on1adapter: release the acquire fence handles it leaks on every
        passthrough camera buffer, otherwise cameraserver runs out of fds.
        The other half is frameworks/av/0005 (cameraservice closes the same
        fences on the client side); both are needed, neither helps alone.

media (frameworks/av)
  0001  NvMM-era display/decode compat for the legacy tegra stack
  0002  codec2: legacy gralloc support (mocha / Tegra K1)
  0003  stagefright: ACodec resolves empty vendor parameters usage
  0004  omx: SoftVideoEncoderOMXComponent lockYCbCr/YV12 fallback, the encoder
        path this board's gralloc needs (same YV12 packing issue as the camera
        HAL, see system/netd and hardware/broadcom for the CFI/stride family)

display (frameworks/native)
  0001  binder: allow the legacy camera interfaces; surfaceflinger: blackout
        logging and forced GPU composition (this board has no working HWC2
        path for the tegra display controller lineup)

power (bootable/recovery)
  0001  charger-mode display fix. minui's fbdev backend flips its double buffer
        with FBIOPUT_VSCREENINFO alone, but tegra_fb_set_par() only takes the
        pixel format, stride and mode out of the var - it never touches
        yoffset and never re-points the display controller. The offset is
        applied in tegra_fb_pan_display(), the FBIOPAN_DISPLAY handler, which
        recomputes win.phys_addr and calls tegra_dc_update_windows(). Without
        it every frame the charger draws lands in a buffer nobody scans out:
        the charger runs, plays the animation and even suspends afterwards,
        while the panel keeps showing what the bootloader left there.
        SetDisplayedFramebuffer() now issues FBIOPAN_DISPLAY as well, and Init()
        unblanks before binding the window, because a pan issued against a
        disabled display controller has nothing to update.
  0002  health HAL 2.1 goes into system (hardware/interfaces/0002). Upstream
        builds android.hardware.health@2.1-service and its passthrough
        implementation as vendor:true modules; this board has no vendor image,
        so they were compiled but never installed and charger mode waited
        forever for android.hardware.health@2.1::IHealth/default. Charger only
        draws once it receives a battery reading, so the screen stayed on the
        bootloader logo with just the charging LED alive. The patch drops
        vendor:true, moves the service path in its .rc to /system/bin/hw and
        lets the VINTF fragment install under /system/etc/vintf/manifest.

build (build/make, system/core, system/sepolicy, prebuilts/gcc, vendor/lineage)
  build/make/0001        non-A/B OTA: keep the rootfs symlinks in the package
  build/make/0002        drop DeviceAsWebcam from handheld_system.mk (a
                         filter-out cannot remove an inherited module, see
                         lineage_mocha.mk for why)
  system/core/0001       init: zygote32 LD_PRELOAD shim
  system/sepolicy/0001   two gaps in the system-private policy this board needs
                         and vendor policy cannot express (both domains live in
                         system/sepolicy/private, so no device-side rule can
                         name them):
                         - linkerconfig reads the NVIDIA /vendor blobs while
                           building the linker namespaces
                         - misctrl reads its own property block (upstream only
                           grants set_prop)
                         This is the only place outside the device tree that
                         touches a system-private domain; everything else is
                         done in device sepolicy.
  prebuilts/gcc/0001     use python3 for the host toolchain wrappers
  vendor/lineage/0001    APN module ownership

system (frameworks/base, lineage-sdk, packages/*, system/netd)
  Small Android 14 API and robustness fixes needed to build and run LineageOS 21
  on this device:
  frameworks/base/0001   power: fix the Notifier NPE on charging state changes
  frameworks/base/0002   settings: add the biometric protected-packages config
  frameworks/base/0003   sqlite: check selection brackets in the tokenizer
  frameworks/base/0004   screenrecord: AVC fallback and encoder accepted size
  frameworks/base/0005   screenrecord: disable the HEVC switch without a HW encoder
  frameworks/base/0006   screenrecord: keep the share dialog buttons on screen
  frameworks/base/0007   transition animation: clamp the screenshot pixel index
                         to the buffer limit. getPixelLuminance() indexes the
                         hardware screenshot buffer raw, and on TN8 that buffer
                         can be larger than the ImageReader limit reports, so the
                         index ran past the limit and threw
                         IndexOutOfBoundsException inside system_server during a
                         configuration change - observed as a boot-time crash
                         loop.
  lineage-sdk/0001       user content observer: match the Android 14 callback
  packages/apps/Glimpse/0001        request/release audio focus for video playback
  packages/apps/Settings/0001       use the Android 14 device-admin and biometric APIs
  packages/apps/Trebuchet/0001      quickstep: match the Android 14 APIs
  packages/modules/adb/0001         adb: stop the legacy FunctionFS transport from
                                    reporting detached while the host is probing
  packages/modules/Connectivity/0001 netbpfload: do not abort on a sysfs subdir
  packages/modules/Connectivity/0002 tethering: default the BPF offload switch off.
                                    Tethering uses BPF maps for NAT since
                                    Android 14, but the 3.10 vendor kernel has no
                                    eBPF at all (nativeBpfFdGet returns ENOSYS),
                                    so BpfCoordinator can never build the
                                    upstream4/downstream4/dev maps. With the
                                    switch off TetherController::setNat()
                                    programs the iptables MASQUERADE chain,
                                    which this kernel does support. See
                                    system/netd/0001 for the other half.
  packages/modules/IntentResolver/0001 intent forwarder: use the Android 14 PM API
  packages/modules/Wifi/0001        wifi: guard a null mWifi to avoid a
                                    system_server crash
  packages/services/Mms/0001        match the Android 14 IMms identity arguments
  system/netd/0001                  tethering: treat the upstream-source filter
                                    (ip6tables -m rpfilter) as best effort.
                                    netd runs it before the forwarding rules and
                                    used to abort with -EREMOTEIO when it fails,
                                    which surfaces as
                                    TETHER_ERROR_ENABLE_FORWARDING_ERROR and
                                    leaves every USB/Wi-Fi tether attempt stuck
                                    in AvailableState. rpfilter only entered
                                    mainline in Linux 3.13; this 3.10 kernel has
                                    no xt_rpfilter, and the rule is anti-spoofing
                                    hardening rather than a forwarding
                                    prerequisite, so log it and continue.
  hardware/broadcom/wlan/0001       wifi-hal: fix the use-after-free in the cleanup
                                    command loop
  hardware/broadcom/wlan/0002       wifi-hal: enable CFI for libwifi-hal-bcm (a
                                    failed CFI check aborts the HAL, which throws
                                    an NPE in system_server and replays the boot
                                    animation)

Working tree notes
------------------

The author keeps the longer explanatory comments in Chinese in the local working
copy; the commits pushed to GitHub carry the English wording. If the tree shows
those files as modified while `git diff` only touches comments, that is expected
- `git checkout -- <file>` restores the committed English version.

One deliberate exception: the comment text inside the patch files in this
directory stays in Chinese. A patch is a byte-exact diff against a source tree,
so its added lines have to match what `install.sh` puts there - and those source
trees (frameworks/av, frameworks/base, ...) are the local working copies that
keep the Chinese notes. Translating a patch body would either stop it from
applying or force the same translation into the third-party tree, which is the
opposite of keeping the notes local. Translate the tree first, regenerate the
patch from `git diff`, and both can change together.

Removed entries and why
-----------------------

Patches whose target change is already present upstream were dropped, because
they blocked a forward application of the series while contributing nothing.
None of the files below exist in this directory any more; the names are kept
only so that an older build log can still be matched:

* frameworks/av/0032-omx-bqhelper...patch.disabled targeted bqhelper files that
  no longer exist in this LineageOS 21 checkout.
* frameworks/av/0034...patch was a 2017 Qualcomm patch for
  OMXNodeInstance::freeNode and was never applied by install.sh.
* hardware/lineage/interfaces/0001, 0003, 0004 were already fully present in
  the checked-out LineageOS 21 source; verified line by line against HEAD.
* hardware/lineage/interfaces/0002...patch.disabled was superseded by the same
  upstream changes.
* packages/modules/adb/0001-hidl-transport...patch.disabled (the earlier HIDL
  rewrite attempt) is superseded by the legacy FunctionFS implementation
  already present in the checked-out source. The currently applied adb fix is
  `0001-adb-fix-legacy-functionfs-offline-transport-detached.patch`, which is
  a different change: it stops the legacy transport from reporting the USB
  endpoint as detached while the host is still probing it.

Every remaining patch was re-verified on 2026-11-28: the series reproduces the
local source tree byte for byte. `uninstall.sh` followed by `install.sh` was run
over all 35 patches and every touched file came back with an identical MD5 sum;
`git status` in frameworks/av and frameworks/native ends up empty, i.e. the
patches rebuild those commits exactly. `check.sh` reports 35/35 APPLIED.

2026-11-28 pass
---------------

* Added `frameworks/base/0007-transition-animation-clamp-screenshot-pixel-index.patch`:
  the boot-time `IndexOutOfBoundsException` in `getPixelLuminance()` was applied
  in the tree but had never been exported, so a fresh `repo sync` plus
  `install.sh` would have lost it and brought the system_server crash loop back.
* Translated the source comments of four patches to English and regenerated them
  from `git diff` (adb transport, Wifi HAL null guard, and both bcmdhd wifi_hal
  patches). Following the policy above the tree was translated first, so patch
  and tree still match exactly.
* Converted the two personal forks into patches: `frameworks/av/0001-0005` and
  `frameworks/native/0001`. The series now reproduces those fork branches
  exactly (verified by tree hash from a clean upstream worktree), so the forks
  are no longer referenced by `.repo/local_manifests` and can be deleted from
  GitHub. FMRadio's fork had no local commits and was unpinned the same way.
* `check.sh` was rewritten to validate against a scratch copy of the touched
  files instead of a per-project throwaway index: about 2 s instead of 3m17s,
  same strictness and the same APPLIED / READY / COVERED / CONFLICT vocabulary.
* No patch turned out to be obsolete: coverage is exact, so nothing was dropped.

Note on frameworks/av/0041
--------------------------

The upstream Qualcomm commit assumed the Mutex::Autolock in
OMXNodeInstance::freeNode() was acquired before the mActiveBuffers check. In
this tree the check does not exist yet, so the patch could only be applied in
reverse. It has been regenerated from the actual tree difference: it now
inserts the mActiveBuffers free before the lock is taken, which is the same
end state that was built and tested on the device.
